# Improved performance and air stability of perovskite solar cells based on low-cost organic hole-transporting material X60 by incorporating its dicationic salt

1State Key Laboratory of Fine Chemicals, Institute of Artificial Photosynthesis, DUT-KTH Joint Education and Research Center on Molecular Devices, Dalian University of Technology (DUT), Dalian 116024, China 2Department of Chemistry, School of Chemical Science and Engineering, KTH Royal Institute of Technology, 100 44 Stockholm, Sweden
The development of an efficient, stable, and low-cost hole-transporting material (HTM) is of great significance for perovskite solar cells (PSCs) from future commercialization point of view. Herein, we specifically synthesize a dicationic salt of X60 termed $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ , and adopt it as an effective and stable “doping” agent to replace the previously used lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) for the low-cost organic HTM X60 in PSCs. The incorporation of this dicationic salt significantly increases the hole conductivity of X60 by two orders of magnitude from $10^{-6}$ to $10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ . The dramatic enhancement of the conductivity leads to an impressive power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $19.0\%$ measured at 1 sun illumination $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $\operatorname{AM}1.5\operatorname{G}$ ), which is comparable to that of the device doped with LiTFSI $(19.3\%)$ under an identical condition. More strikingly, by replacing LiTFSI, the PSC devices incorporating $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ also show an excellent long-term durability under ambient atmosphere for 30 days, mainly due to the hydrophobic nature of the $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ doped HTM layer, which can effectively prevent the moisture destroying the perovskite layer. The present work paves the way for the development of highly efficient, stable, and low-cost HTM for potential commercialization of PSCs.
perovskite solar cells, hole-transporting materials, X60, stability, sustainable energy
Citation: Qu J, Jiang X, Yu Z, Lai J, Zhao Y, Hu M, Yang X, Sun L. Improved performance and air stability of perovskite solar cells based on low-cost organi hole-transporting material X60 by incorporating its dicationic salt. Sci China Chem, 2017, 60, 10.1007/s11426-017-9141-9
## 1  Introduction
Organic-inorganic metal halide perovskites with a general chemical formula of $\mathrm{ABX}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(A{=}C H_{3}N H_{3}{'}}$ (MA), $\mathrm{NH}{=}\mathrm{CHNH}_{3}{}^{+}$ (FA) or Cs; $\scriptstyle\mathbf{B=Pb}$ or Sn; $\scriptstyle\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{C}1$ , Br, I) have been intensively studied as light-harvesting materials in solid-state thin-film solar cells recently, owing to some of their excellent optoelectronic properties, such as direct band gap, small exciton binding energy, a broad absorption range, high optical absorption coefficient, high charge carrier mobility and long charge carrier diffusion length etc. [1−6]. The highest power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has been reached a certified value of $22.1\%$ in only few years [7]. Perovskite materials are quite cheap and they can be fabricated by a simple solution-processable method. The excellent photovoltaic performance coupled with the ease of the fabrication processes makes PSCs as a promising low-cost alternative to replace the conventional
silicon-based solar cells.
In a state-of-the-art regular mesoscopic PSC, perovskite absorber is penetrated into the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ scaffold and formed with a capping layer, which is further coated with a thin layer of hole-transporting material (HTM). HTM plays a vital role in effectively extracting and transporting the photo-generated holes from the perovskite to the back contact metal electrode, thus determining the overall performance [8,9]. Small molecule $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di- $p$ -methoxyphenylamine)- ${\cdot9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) has been the most widely used HTM in PSCs so far, and has shown excellent PCE of over $21\%$ [10]. However, the synthesis of spiro-OMeTAD is quite complex, including five reaction steps with a low total production yield of less than $40\%$ [11,12]. Moreover, highly efficient PSC devices also require high-purity sublimation-grade spiro-OMeTAD [13]. As a consequence, the price of spiro-OMeTAD is extremely high (more than 10 times that of gold), which severely restricts its future large-scale applicaition [14]. Therefore, it is highly desirable to develop low-cost and highly efficient alternative HTMs to substitute spiro-OMeTAD in PSCs from potential commercialization point of view. With this regard, a number of alternative low-cost HTMs have been developed in PSCs, including small molecules [15–21], conducting polymers [22,23], and inorganic p-type semiconductors [14,24–26].
Among small molecule-based HTMs studied, only few examples have achieved competitive PCEs, which could rival spiro-OMeTAD [15–17] $N2,N2,N2^{\prime},N2^{\prime},N7,N7,N7^{\prime},N7^{\prime}$ octakis(4-methoxyphenyl)spiro[fluorene- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -xanthene]- $^{2,2^{\prime},7}$ , $7^{\prime}$ -tetraamine coded as X60 has been one of the most successful representatives, showing an impressive PCE of $19.84\%$ under $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ( $\mathrm{\AM}\ 1.5\mathrm{G})$ solar irradiation, which was close to that of spiro-OMeTAD measured under an identical condition [17]. More strikingly, by stark contrast to the tedious synthesis of spiro-OMeTAD, X60 was synthesized by only a simple two-step reaction route with a high overall yield of over $70\%$ . The estimated synthesis cost of X60 is more than 5 times lower than spiro-OMeTAD [27]. Thus, the high performance and low-production cost render X60 as a promising alternative HTM for commercial application of PSCs. In the previous work, LiTFSI was incorporated to create free carriers and to increase the conductivity, therefore resulting in a higher overall efficiency [28]. However, it has been reported that lithium salt is hydroscopic, which has a negative impact on the long-term stability of PSC device [28].
In this work, we specifically synthesized a dicationic salt of X60 termed $X60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ (Figure 1), and adopted it as a “doping” agent to replace the previously used LiTFSI for the low-cost organic HTM X60 in PSCs, since the incorporation of an oxidized form of spiro-type small molecule has been proved to be an effective strategy to enhance the conductivity and thus the overall efficiency [29,30]. The introduction of $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ substantially enhanced the conductivity of X60, which led to an impressive PCE of up to $19.0\%$ at 1 sun illumination $\mathrm{(AM~}1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ , $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . This efficiency can rival the corresponding value obtained by doping with LiTFSI $(19.3\%)$ under the same condition. More intriguingly, the PSC devices incorporating $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ also showed a better long-term durability under ambient atmosphere as compared to the LiTFSI counterparts.
## 2  Experimental
## 2.1  Synthesis of $\mathbf{X60(TFSI)}_{2}$
$N2,N2,N2^{\prime},N2^{\prime},N7,N7,N7^{\prime},N7^{\prime}.$ -octakis(4-methoxyphenyl)spiro[fluorene- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ -xanthene]- $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetraamine (X60, Polymer Light Technology, China) $\left(1~\mathrm{g},\ 0.8\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ ) and silver bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (AgTFSI, Aladdin, China) $(0.7\mathrm{g},1.8\mathrm{mmol})$ were mixed into $100~\mathrm{mL}$ degassed dichloromethane. The solution was purged and backfilled with nitrogen for several times and then stirred at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The reaction was washed with dichloromethane and then filtered to remove the white power. Dark-purple solid was obtained by a rotary evaporator at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove the solvent. Then the solid was washed by dry diethyl ether and recovered by filtration for 3 times. The final power was dried under vacuum to afford a dark-purple solid with a total yield of $72\%$ ( $\cdot1.04\ \mathrm{g},0.57\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ). Elemental analysis calcd $(\%)$ for $\mathrm{C_{85}H_{68}F_{12}N_{6}O_{17}S_{4}}$ : C, 56.66; H, 3.80; N, 4.46; S, 7.12. Found: C, 54.36; H, 3.86; N, 4.63; S, 7.28.
## 2.2  Device fabrication and characterization
All chemicals and reagents were purchased from chemical companies unless otherwise statement. $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ $(>98\%)$ was from TCI (Japan); $\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{2}$ $(99\%)$ , HI ( $48\%$ in water), HBr 1 $48\%$ in water), $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $33\ \mathrm{\wt\%}$ in absolute ethanol), formamidine acetate $(99\%)$ , 4-tert-butylpyridine (TBP, $96\%$ ), titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylactonate) $75\%$ in isopropanol (Tiacac) and Li-bis(trifluoromethane-sulfonyl) imide (LiTFSI) were from Sigma-Aldrich (USA); and mesoporous- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ paste (18NR-T) were from Dyesol (Australia). The $\mathrm{\DeltaNH{=}C H N H_{3}I}$ (FAI) and $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}B r}$ (MABr) were prepared according to a previous Ref. [11]. The mixed-ion perovskite precursor solution was prepared by mixing MABr $(0.2{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~M}}})$ , FAI (1 M), $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ $(0.2\ \mathrm{M})$ , and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (1.1 M) in a mixed solvent of dimethyl formamide (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) $(4{:}1,\nu/\nu)$ for a final structure of $({\mathrm{FAPbI}}_{3})_{1-x}({\mathrm{MAPbBr}}_{3})_{x}$ $(x{=}0.15)$ ) [31,32].
The photocurrent-voltage $(J{-}V)$ characteristics of the PSC devices were measured by using a Keithley 2400 Sourcemeasure (USA) unit under $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ illumination, AM $1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ , provided by an Oriel Sol3A solar simulator (Model:
94023A; Newport, USA) with an $\mathbf{AM}1.5$ filter in ambient air. Light intensity was calibrated by a Newport calibrated standard Si reference cell (SER. No: 506/0358, USA). Square solar cell with an active area of $0.20\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ was covered by a black mask with a circular aperture of $0.09\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The $J_{-}V$ curves were achieved from forward bias to short-circuit at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{S}^{-1}$ . The incident photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra were recorded by Hypermono-light (SM-25, Jasco Co. Ltd., Japan). A standard silicon solar cell was used as calibration before the measurement.
## 2.3 Characterizations
The UV/Vis absorption spectra were measured in dichloromethane with a concentration of $3{\times}10^{-5}$ M by Agilent 8453 spectrophotometer (Model: HP 8435, China). Redox potentials of X60 were measured in dichloromethane with $0.1\mathrm{MTBAPF}_{6}$ as a supporting electrolyte. The working electrode was a glassy carbon electrode with a surface area of $0.785\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . A platinum wire and $\mathrm{Ag/AgNO_{3}}$ electrode were used as counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. The redox potential was calibrated against $\mathrm{Fc/Fc^{+}}$ as an internal standard. The cross-section scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained by HR-SEM performed with FEI (Field Emission Instruments: Nova Nano SEM 450, USA). Conductivity measurements were performed according to a previously reported method [35]. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were performed at different applied bias voltages under 1 sun illumination $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $\mathrm{AM}\ 1.5\ \mathrm{G},$ ) using an impedance/gain phase analyser (Zahner Model: Zennium, Serial No. 40037, Germany) electrochemical workstation with the scanning frequency range from $10^{6}$ to $0.1\mathrm{Hz}$ . The magnitude of the alternative signal was $10~\mathrm{mV}.$
## 3  Results and discussion
$\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ was synthesized by oxidizing X60 with silver bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (AgTFSI) via one step reaction with a total yield of $72\%$ as shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information online). Dark-purple powder was obtained after purification. $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ showed good solubility in common organic solvents (chlorobenzene or dichlorobenzene), so that it can be added into the HTM solution with a desirable amount. More details of the synthetic procedures can be found in the Experimental section.
UV/Vis absorption spectra of X60 containing different molar concentrations of $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ in dichloromethane solution are shown in Figure 2(a). Pristine X60 shows a maximum absorption peak at about $382\mathrm{nm}$ . The gradual addition of $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ results in an obvious decrease of the absorption peak at $382\mathrm{nm}$ . Meanwhile, a new absorption peak arises at around $523{\mathrm{nm}}$ , corresponding to the singly oxidized X60 as observed previously [17]. It indicates that X60 is partially oxidized to its radical cation when mixed with $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ . Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were carried out to evaluate the oxidation potentials of pristine X60 and its oxidized form (Figures S2 and S3). The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level of X60 can be calculated by the following equation: $e E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}{=}{-}5.1{-}(E_{\mathrm{ox}}{-}E^{1/2}(\mathrm{Fc}/\mathrm{Fc}^{+}))$ , where $E_{\mathrm{ox}}$ stands for the onset oxidation potential (vs.
$E^{1/2}(\mathrm{Fc}/\mathrm{Fc}^{+}))$ [33]. The HOMO energy level of X60 is thus determined to be $-5.12\mathrm{eV},$ which agrees well the previously reported value [17]. The ionization potential of $\mathrm{X}60^{+}$ is estimated to be $-5.30\mathrm{eV}.$
The hole conductivities of X60 with varied molar ratios of $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ were evaluated by using a two-contact electrical conductivity setup as reported previously [31,35]. As presented in Figures $2(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{b}},\ \mathsf{c})$ , pristine X60 shows a rather low conductivity of only about $1.96{\times}10^{-6}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ . As expected, the introduction of $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ indeed dramatically enhances the conductivity of $\mathrm{X}60\ \mathrm{fim}$ . Upon addition of $8\mathrm{\mol\%}$ $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ , the conductivity value is increased to $1.72{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which reaches the same level as the one doped with LiTFSI [17]. The results demonstrate that $X60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ can effectively enhance the hole conductivity, largely due to the increase of the charge carrier density. Hole conductivity is an essential parameter to ensure sufficient hole collections and thus to determine the overall photovoltaic performance of the PSC devices.
The photovoltaic performance of PSCs with various concentrations of $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ was further evaluated with a cell structure of FTO glass/compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite/HTM/Au. The cell architecture and the corresponding energy level diagram in PSCs are depicted in Figures 3(a, b). Cross-sectional SEM image of the PSCs (Figure 3(c)) displays a well-defined layer-by-layer structure. The mixed-ion perovskite light absorber $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ , which was prepared by a previously reported solvent-engineering method [32,36], infiltrated inside the pores of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ scaffold $(\sim150\:\mathrm{nm})$ and formed a capping layer with a total thickness of ${\sim}600\mathrm{nm}$ . The obtained perovskite film was further coated by a HTM layer $(\sim200\mathrm{nm})$ and Au $(\sim80\mathrm{nm})$ . The detailed fabrication processes of PSCs are described in the Experimental section.
The current density-voltage $\left(J\mathbf{-}V\right)$ characteristics of PSCs with different concentrations of $X60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ measured at $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ illumination $\mathbf{\Sigma}_{\mathrm{AM}\ 1.5\ \mathrm{~G~}}$ are shown in Figure 4(a) and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are listed in Table 1. The best PSC device based on pristine X60 exhibits a rather low PCE of merely $3.7\%$ , with a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $12.3\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , an open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of $0.99{\mathrm{V}}$ and a fill factor (FF) of 0.30, respectively. All the photovoltaic parameters are significantly enhanced after adding $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ , especially for the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and FF. Under an optimal condition $(16\mathrm{\mol\%})$ , a maximum PCE of up to $19.0\%$ is achieved, with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $23.6\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of 1.05 V, and a FF of 0.76. This doping concentration was chosen for the following comparisons. The photovoltaic performance of PSC devices employing LiTFSI as an additive was also studied under identical conditions for comparison purpose (Figure S4). The best device exhibits a comparable PCE of $19.3\%$ relative to $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ .
The  incident   photon-to-current   conversion   efficiency (IPCE) spectra of PSCs with and without $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ were measured as presented in Figure 4(b). Device with $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ clearly displays much higher IPCE values in the whole region measured, with a broad plateau of over $80\%$ in the range of $400{-}750~\mathrm{nm}$ . This result emphasizes that $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ doped devices effectively transport and collect holes from the perovskite to the back contact electrode, leading to a higher charge-collection efficiency and thus higher $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ . Figure 4(c) displays the histogram of 30 identical PSC devices containing $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ , showing a good reproducibility and yielding an average PCE of $18.5\%$ . The steady-state efficiency of a representative PSC device based on $X60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ was also tested at a fixed bias voltage of $0.9\mathrm{V}$ for 180 s under one sun illumination 1 $\mathrm{100~mW~cm^{-2}}$ , $\operatorname{AM}1.5\operatorname{G}$ ), as shown in Figure 4(d). It shows a steady-state efficiency of $17.2\%$ and a current density of $19.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively.
The long-term stability of PSCs containing either $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ or LiTFSI were also investigated for 30 days. The devices were kept without encapsulation at an ambient condition with humidity of ${\sim}30\%$ in the dark. Figure 6(a) presents the dynamic changes of the photovoltaic parameters for the representative PSC devices. Device with $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ exhibited an excellent long-term stability and maintained over $90\%$ of its initial PCE. By stark contrast, device with LiTFSI remained only about $70\%$ of the initial PCE during the testing period. Figure 6(c) shows the contact angles of X60 films with $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ and LiTFSI. $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ doped film shows a larger angle $(96^{\circ})$ than that of LiTFSI $(85.5^{\circ})$ . The water contact angles of X60 films  remained  almost  no change before and after X60(TFSI)2 doping (Figure S5 and Figure 6(b)). The hydrophobic characteristic of HTM containing $X60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ can effectively prevent the moisture penetrating into the perovskite film, giving rise to a better longterm durability. As shown in Figure 6(c), the device with $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ remained its original dark brown color during the testing period.
## 4  Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully introduced $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ as an effective and stable “doping” agent to replace LiTFSI for the low-cost organic HTM X60 in PSCs. The incorporation of this dicationic salt significantly increased the hole conductivity of X60, which led to an impressive PCE of $19.0\%$ measured at 1 sun illumination $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , AM 1.5 G). More strikingly, the PSC devices incorporating $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ also showed a good air stability under ambient atmosphere, mainly due to the hydrophobic nature of the $\mathrm{X}60(\mathrm{TFSI})_{2}$ doped HTM layer, which can effectively prevent the moisture destroying the perovskite film. The present work offers an effective strategy for developing highly efficient, stable, and low-cost HTMs in PSCs.
